WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Arsenic is incredibly toxic.

2
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.439
For centuries, until modern methods of detection were discovered, murderers found it to be

3
00:00:15.439 --> 00:00:18.100
a very popular substance.

4
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:24.239
So you can imagine the shock at the 48th meeting of the German Association of Arts and Sciences

5
00:00:24.239 --> 00:00:31.200
in 1875, when two men sat in the front of the audience and downed more than double a

6
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.720
deadly dose of arsenic.

7
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.599
The next day, the men were back at the conference, smiling and healthy.

8
00:00:38.599 --> 00:00:44.959
How is it possible to take something so bad for you and stay alive, and even look healthy

9
00:00:44.959 --> 00:00:49.639
despite the damage being done at a microscopic level to your body?

10
00:00:49.639 --> 00:00:54.400
The answer has an uncanny relationship to procrastination.

11
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.040
That's what we're going to be talking about in the next few videos.

12
00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.439
You've already learned one handy tool to help you with procrastination.

13
00:01:02.439 --> 00:01:10.440
The Pomodoro, that 25-minute period of uninterrupted focus, followed by a bit of relaxation.

14
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.199
This week, we're going to learn more.

15
00:01:13.199 --> 00:01:18.160
Understanding a little about the cognitive psychology of procrastination, just like understanding

16
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:23.519
the chemistry of poison, can help us develop healthy preventatives.

17
00:01:23.519 --> 00:01:30.040
In these videos, I'm going to teach you the lazy person's approach to tackling procrastination.

18
00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:35.800
This means you'll be learning about your inner zombies, the routine, habitual responses

19
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.120
your brain falls into as a result of specific cues.

20
00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:45.279
These zombie responses are often focused on making the here and now better.

21
00:01:45.279 --> 00:01:50.480
As you'll see, you can trick some of these zombies into helping you fend off procrastination

22
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:51.480
when you need to.

23
00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.720
Not all procrastination is bad.

24
00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.680
Even if you're pretty good already in handling procrastination, you'll learn some helpful

25
00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:04.720
insights here that can allow you to better prioritize your learning.

26
00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.600
The reason that learning to avoid procrastination is so important is that good learning is a

27
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.160
bit-by-bit activity.

28
00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:17.039
You want to avoid cramming, which doesn't build solid neural structures.

29
00:02:17.039 --> 00:02:22.000
By putting the same amount of time into your learning, but spacing that learning out by

30
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.479
starting earlier, you'll learn better.

31
00:02:25.479 --> 00:02:31.440
First things first, unlike procrastination, which is easy to fall into, willpower is hard

32
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:32.639
to come by.

33
00:02:32.639 --> 00:02:35.800
It uses a lot of neural resources.

34
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:41.000
You shouldn't waste willpower on fending off procrastination except when absolutely

35
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.360
necessary.

36
00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.000
Best of all, as you'll see, you don't need to.

37
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.979
If you'll remember, we procrastinate about things that make us feel a little bit uncomfortable.

38
00:02:51.979 --> 00:02:57.479
You think about something you don't particularly like, and the pain centers of your brain light

39
00:02:57.479 --> 00:03:03.160
up, so you shift and narrow your focus of attention to something more enjoyable.

40
00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.440
This causes you to feel better.

41
00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.320
At least temporarily.

42
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:12.800
But sadly, the long-term effects of habitual avoidance can be nasty.

43
00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.800
When you put off your studies, it can become even more painful to think about studying

44
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:18.800
it.

45
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.880
You can choke on tests because you haven't laid the firm neural foundations you need

46
00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.559
to feel comfortable with the material.

47
00:03:26.559 --> 00:03:32.759
Procrastination can be a single, monumentally important, keystone bad habit.

48
00:03:32.759 --> 00:03:38.919
A habit, in other words, that influences many important areas of your life.

49
00:03:38.919 --> 00:03:44.960
If you improve your abilities in this area, many other positive changes will gradually

50
00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:46.960
begin to unfold.

51
00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.440
Procrastination shares features with addiction.

52
00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.839
It offers temporary excitement and relief from sometimes boring reality.

53
00:03:54.839 --> 00:04:00.080
It's easy to fool yourself, for example, into thinking that the best use of any given

54
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:05.679
moment is surfing the web for information instead of actually reading the textbook or

55
00:04:05.679 --> 00:04:07.839
doing the assigned problems.

56
00:04:07.839 --> 00:04:10.000
You start to tell yourself stories.

57
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:15.800
For example, you might tell yourself that organic chemistry requires spatial reasoning.

58
00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:19.559
You're a weakness, so of course you're doing very poorly at it.

59
00:04:19.559 --> 00:04:26.239
You devise irrational excuses that sound superficially reasonable, like, if I study too far ahead

60
00:04:26.239 --> 00:04:29.359
of the test, I'll forget the material.

61
00:04:29.359 --> 00:04:34.239
If you're troubled by procrastination, you may even start telling yourself that procrastination

62
00:04:34.239 --> 00:04:38.040
is an innate, unchangeable characteristic.

63
00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:43.119
After all, if procrastination were easily fixable, wouldn't you have fixed it by now?

64
00:04:43.119 --> 00:04:48.000
The higher you go in your studies, however, the more important it is to take control of

65
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.119
procrastination.

66
00:04:50.119 --> 00:04:54.160
Habits that worked in earlier years can turn around and bite you.

67
00:04:54.160 --> 00:05:00.640
What I'll show you in these next few videos is how you can become the master of your habits.

68
00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:06.440
You should be making the decisions not your well-meaning, but unthinking zombies, your

69
00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.959
habits.

70
00:05:07.959 --> 00:05:13.000
As you'll see, the strategies for dealing with procrastination are simple.

71
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.799
It's just that sometimes they aren't intuitively obvious.

72
00:05:17.799 --> 00:05:21.079
So let's return to that story that began this video.

73
00:05:21.079 --> 00:05:25.440
The arsenic eaters started with tiny doses of arsenic.

74
00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.239
In tiny doses, arsenic doesn't seem harmful.

75
00:05:29.239 --> 00:05:33.440
You can even build up an immunity to its effects.

76
00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:39.359
This can allow you to take larger doses and look healthy, even as the poison is slowly

77
00:05:39.359 --> 00:05:44.399
increasing your risk of cancer and ravaging your organs.

78
00:05:44.399 --> 00:05:49.959
In a similar way, procrastinators put off just that one little thing.

79
00:05:49.959 --> 00:05:54.440
They do it again and again, gradually growing used to it.

80
00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:58.760
They can even look healthy, but the long-term effects?

81
00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:00.200
Not so good.

82
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:01.959
I'm Barbara Oakley.

83
00:06:01.959 --> 00:06:03.640
Thanks for learning how to learn.